This file is for the design of the control bandwidth verification workflow.

Goals: Outline a measurement method which can accurately measure the control performance of the motor. This method must measure the control bandwidth, and ideally would be extensible in a few directions. One is to be used as a general device verification method, meaning that all the key device parameters should be measured. Another extension to keep in mind is automation -- can the measurement be performed within a few minutes per chip without significant operator intervention? For now, we will leave automation aside but continue to consider general driver verification.

Background: It will be helpful to outline examples for macroscopic hardware device verification (like a 3d printer or other stepper/servo) so that we can build an analogous concept for our nanodevice. Control methods can be open- or closed-loop, which refers to whether information about the output is fed back into the driver to determine the input. Closed-loop methods are typically much more accurate due to the additional information the driver has to determine the correct input to match the correct output. If a closed-loop control is used, the measurement method must be present during verification, as we should determine if the device is performing in the correct environment. For most modern electric motors, this feedback measurement is done by an (optical) encoder. That encoder feeds position and velocity data back to the driver to perform the closed-loop measurement. In this case, our position is an angular position of the end of the lever relative to the device surface. In order to assess the control bandwidth usefully, we need to determine that the encoder, if present, is working properly, such that the control bandwidth measurement is valid.

We should envision what kind of feedback is possible in our case. One potential method is to use capacitance from a location near the fulcrum. Whatever the method, I will refer to it generally as an encoder. In this case, we could potentially perform orthogonal verification on an optical microscope to "double check" the encoder and driver functions.

Aside: Encoder architectures
There is an interesting challenge with using capacitance as an encoder which we will need to track. For macroscopic encoders, their measurement is linear with their position measuremeent, which means that small errors in the encoder will lead to small errors in the position measurement, and that the errors are expected to occur to a similar degree across the range of motion (RoM). With capacitance, this is not exactly the case. Capacitance is not a linear function (1/d, not d) of angular position and there will be some areas where encoder sensitivity is much higher than others. This will lead to erratic behavior at high drive frequencies depending on how many high-confidence measurements are made in the sensitive region (close to the surface). It is possible that the encoder will be suitably accurate over the range of motion, but capacitance will be affected by buffer composition, drive voltage from the neighboring elements, and environmental emf. See https://elm-chan.org/works/vlp/report_e.html for more details on design considerations for capacitative encoders, or https://www.electromate.com/news/post/comparing-capacitive-and-inductive-encoders for a comparison with inductive encoders.
Note that the capacitative encoders used the overlapping area of two rotating elements (making capacitance linear with angle!) rather than distance.

Inductive encoders are also commercially available, and gold nanoparticles have been used to implement nanoscale inductors with DNA origami before. https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aeb2001 Due to the current architecture using a gold nanoparticle for the fulcrum, it is unclear how design elements could be engineered to permit inductive sensing of the angle, though. I considered non-spherical fulcrum elements but it is unclear how to make or purchase them to maintain their orientation during device assembly. It would be potentially very interesting to engineer the fulcrum so that it can also act as an encoder.

The last method I think is very promising is magnetic sensing. Magnetism is used in nanoscale sensing in both hard-drives and in the development of diamond sensors. Our setup is potentially compatible with a hard-drive schema, in which the TMR (tunneling magneto-resistance) effect is created by a tiny device grown onto the chip surface. This would significantly increase chip design complexity but potentially offer a very robust feedback method for control. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunnel_magnetoresistance and see the page on magnetic encoders for more information.

Measurement method:
A central question to the control bandwidth measurement is what the orthogonal measurement method is, as referenced in the goal. There are several options leveraging different elements of the system, and we should favor those which are well-characterized in their methods and approach, as it will be easier to establish a ground truth.

AFM: One potential method for drive verification is to use AFM to detect deflection in the lever arm. A high-frequency scan or stationary measurement of a loaded tip could potentially produce measurements of drive output. However, AFM in a scanning mode is limited to 10 Hz or so, which is much too slow. In a pulling mode, we could potentially measure faster, but measuring a displacement under load is not the intended use for our device. I'm not sure if AFM is particularly compatible with this.

TIRF microscopy: Another method to measure the device output orthogonally would be to perform TIRF microscopy and convert the position of the lever to an intensity. One challenge would be to get the evanescent field oriented and positioned near the device surface. Devices can be patterned onto glass so that we can image through the substrate but this doesn't seem like a desirable approach. Shot noise would likely limit our acquisition speed to 1 Khz at best, even with 

Confocal microscopy/STORM: Typical optical microscopes have very sharp through-focus performance curves, meaning they can resolve motion in the z-axis to within a few hundred nanometers. This is somewhat too large for our desired spatial resolution 

Open-loop verification of the device:
Prior to engaging closed loop control, we should determine that the device is working as expected.
Closed-loop verification and control bandwidth measurement:
Assuming a suitable choice of encoder, let's outline how we could measure the control bandwidth. Generally speaking, control bandwidth is the range of input frequencies over which the output signal has a loss of 3dB or less, although this is a convention and subject to device requirements. If we take position or velocity as outputs, we could theoretically determine the bandwidth spec by propogating the position or velocity error in the most difficult case. This is not typically done due to a desire for control and verification stability, which is to say that we would like clarity that a device will perform despite the presence of shot noise, operator or instrument differences, etc.
